                    <h6 class="font-bold">Overweight & Obesity</h6>
                    <p class="text-center font-light">Rising rapidly because of changing dietary patterns, sedentary lifestyles, and increased consumption of processed foods (FAO et al. 2025).</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p>These trends are also evident across Southeast Asia. The Asia and the Pacific Regional Overview of Food Security and Nutrition 2025 reports that although child stunting has declined over the past two decades, rates remain above global nutrition targets in many countries. Anaemia among women of reproductive age continues to be a major public health concern, while overweight and obesity are rising rapidly because of changing dietary patterns, sedentary lifestyles, and increased consumption of processed foods (FAO et al. 2025).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-[20ppx] pb-[40px]">
            <h4 class="text-display-24 font-semibold">Climate change and geopolitical instability threaten progress</h4>
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-[20px] font-light">
                <p>The report warns that climate extremes, geopolitical conflicts, and trade disruptions could reverse recent gains. Rising energy prices continue to increase fertilizer, transportation, and food production costs, while extreme weather events reduce agricultural productivity and disrupt food supply chains. IFAD estimates that prolonged geopolitical disruptions could push an additional 9–18 million people into hunger (FAO et al. 2026).</p>
                <p>For Southeast Asia, these risks are particularly acute. The region is highly exposed to typhoons, droughts, floods, sea-level rise, and saltwater intrusion, all of which threaten rice production, fisheries, and rural livelihoods. Climate change therefore poses not only an environmental challenge but also a significant threat to regional food and nutrition security by reducing agricultural productivity, disrupting livelihoods, and hindering progress toward food security and poverty reduction across Asia and the Pacific (ADB 2024).</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-[20ppx] pb-[40px]">
            <h4 class="text-display-24 font-semibold">Agrifood systems transformation is now imperative</h4>
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-[20px] font-light">
                <p>Taken together, the findings of SOFI 2026 point to a fundamental conclusion: ending hunger requires transforming agrifood systems, not merely increasing agricultural production.</br>
                    The report calls for integrated investments in climate-smart agriculture, diversified food production, nutrition-sensitive value chains, resilient infrastructure, digital technologies, agricultural research and innovation, social protection, and reduced food loss and waste (FAO et al. 2026).</p>
                <p>For Southeast Asia, these priorities strongly align with the ASEAN Food, Agriculture and Forestry Sectoral Plan (FAF-SP) 2026–2030, which identifies food and nutrition security, climate resilience, sustainable agriculture, digital transformation, innovation, and regional cooperation as strategic priorities for achieving a resilient and sustainable agrifood sector (ASEAN 2025).</p>
                <p>The evidence therefore suggests that Southeast Asia possesses both the policy frameworks and institutional mechanisms needed to accelerate agrifood systems transformation. The challenge now lies in translating regional commitments into coordinated national actions that improve livelihoods, nutrition, and resilience.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>